# The actor's left-facing run body: 10 frames, complete.
#
# The counterpart of the already-named CV_FIGURE_RUN_RIGHT_BODY_F00..F07,
# and drawn separately rather than mirrored -- none of these ten is
# byte-identical to any right-facing body.
#
# Six frames come straight from the routine names, and the two stages agree
# once the measured base is applied (CAPSULE 0, CAVES 1):
#
#   CAPSULE_ACTOR_RUN_LEFT_DRAW_0,2,3,5,7,8
#   CAVES_ACTOR_RUN_LEFT_PHASE_1,3,4,6,8,9   -> the same frames 0,2,3,5,7,8
#
# Both stages omit 1, 4, 6 and 9, so the gap is in the labelling. The four
# were placed by the same "forced by geometry" standard README.md used for
# its frame-7 gaps -- in each offset gap there is EXACTLY ONE live piece with
# a body shape (5 leaves, 54..58 tall, top -56):
#
#   F01 CV_2E0A   F04 CV_2FD6   F06 CV_3066   F09 CV_30BA
#
# and the cycle's own arithmetic confirms all four. The widths run
#
#   F00..F04   28.5  23.0  30.5  39.5  41.0
#   F05..F09   28.5  23.0  30.5  39.5  41.0
#
# two identical half-strides, with each placed frame landing on the width its
# partner five frames away already had. Offsets are strictly ascending across
# all ten, in frame order.
#
# Reachability was checked before naming, after CV_5F74..CV_5FFC turned out
# to be art behind an always-true guard: all three gap-filled pieces are
# drawn six times each at the actor slot and none sits behind such a guard.
CV_2D76 CV_FIGURE_RUN_LEFT_BODY_F00
